The rows are printed in order by recursion. One helper prints the letters in the current row, and another call moves to the next row with a smaller starting letter.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern18(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        char last = (char) ('A' + n - 1);
        printRows(n, last, last);
    }

    private void printRows(int rowsLeft, char start, char last) {
        if (rowsLeft == 0) {
            return;
        }

        printLetters(start, last);
        System.out.println();
        printRows(rowsLeft - 1, (char) (start - 1), last);
    }

    private void printLetters(char current, char last) {
        System.out.print(current);
        if (current < last) {
            System.out.print(' ');
            printLetters((char) (current + 1), last);
        }
    }
}
```

The important stopping cases are `rowsLeft == 0` for the rows and `current == last` for the letters. With `n = 1`, the only output is `A`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for all recursive calls that print the pattern.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursion stack.

The row starts with a letter that moves backward, while the last letter stays the same as the bottom row. For `n = 5`, the first row starts at `E`, the next at `D`, and so on.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern18(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            char start = 'A' + n - row - 1;
            char end = 'A' + n - 1;

            for (char letter = start; letter <= end; letter++) {
                cout << letter;
                if (letter < end) {
                    cout << ' ';
                }
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 5`, the rows are `E`, `D E`, `C D E`, `B C D E`, and `A B C D E`. An input of `0` produces no output. The pattern assumes the size stays within the uppercase alphabet.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the total number of printed letters is triangular.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output stream.

Each row is built from its first character up to the final character. The first character is found with `n - row - 1`, so it moves from the bottom letter back toward `A`.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern18(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            StringBuilder line = new StringBuilder();
            char first = (char) ('A' + n - row - 1);
            char last = (char) ('A' + n - 1);

            for (char letter = first; letter <= last; letter++) {
                if (line.length() > 0) {
                    line.append(' ');
                }
                line.append(letter);
            }

            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

A quick dry run with `n = 3` gives starts `C`, `B`, and `A`. The output becomes:

```text
C
B C
A B C
```

The `StringBuilder` only holds one row, so it does not store the complete pattern. For `n <= 0`, the loop is skipped.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current row.

Instead of calculating each row's ending point separately, the code keeps one fixed ending offset. The start offset begins at `n - 1` and decreases after every printed line.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern18(int n) {
        int endOffset = n - 1;
        int startOffset = endOffset;

        while (startOffset >= 0) {
            int current = startOffset;

            while (current <= endOffset) {
                cout << static_cast<char>('A' + current);
                if (current != endOffset) {
                    cout << ' ';
                }
                current++;
            }

            cout << '\n';
            startOffset--;
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 3`, `startOffset` takes the values `2`, `1`, and `0`, which prints `C`, then `B C`, then `A B C`. The `while` loops also naturally handle `n = 0`, since the first condition is false.

The input is not changed, and only a few counters are used.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` excluding printed output.

